\DOC LOCAL_SUB_TWICE4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB_TWICE4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal past an inlined modular-subtraction
subroutine, replacing the current machine-state assumptions with ones about the
successor state and adding assumptions characterising the computed difference.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index used to locate the call site within
the enclosing code; the second is a {string list} naming the result and the two
operand variables (destination, minuend, subtrahend), whose assumptions
describe the subtraction performed. The conclusion retains its
{eventually}-style form, except that {t} applied to the previous state becomes
{t} applied to its successor. In the assumptions, every fact about the old
state — program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory reads, and
the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — is discarded and re-asserted for the new
state, with the program counter advanced and the {MAYCHANGE} relation extended
by the components written by the subroutine. A new memory-read assumption
equating the destination region to the named result variable is added, together
with one or two arithmetic assumptions bounding that result and relating it
modulo the prime to the difference of the two named operands (sometimes stated
as implications conditioned on bounds for the operands).
{
         A ?- t
      ====================  LOCAL_SUB_TWICE4_TAC n [dest; minuend; subtrahend]
         A' ?- t'
}
\noindent Here {A'} replaces the old state's facts by the new state's and adds
the result's read and arithmetic assumptions, and {t'} is {t} with the state
variable advanced.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single kind of transformation, on goals in
symbolic execution of this one style.

\ENDDOC
